{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787060457,"nonce":"o2JvyclIcCWPqiMQvtwPswmcnLg=","message":"Done. Migration `00055_suppression_is_an_axis.sql`.\n\n`alerts_state_ck` now admits `firing | resolved | expired`. `suppression_reason` and `suppressed_by` are columns on `alerts`, guarded by `alerts_supreason_ck` (Alertmanager's four reasons only — never `snoozed`) and `alerts_suppress_ck` (a reason requires `state='firing'`). The backfill joins the current case and raises a named EXCEPTION on a row the join misses, rather than inventing a witness. `alerts_open_idx` states liveness as `WHERE state = 'firing'` — the disjunction it has carried since 00007 is gone.\n\nThe conceptual shift: **Suppressed is now a SUBSET of Firing, not a sibling.** `AlertRollup.RollupState()` and `grouping.Counts.Live()` were re-spelled for it.\n\n**No contract change was needed.** `Alert.DisplayState()` preserves the four-value reading for the API, so no DTO, OpenAPI, generated client or web change fell out of a state-machine change — which is the outcome this ticket wanted and did not assume.\n\nReaders found beyond the four this ticket named: `notification/repository/reminders.go:68` (it excluded silenced alerts by accident; now it says so on purpose) and three copy-pasted display derivations in `notification/repository/snapshot.go`, `enrichment/repository/alertread.go` and `drill/repository/artifacts.go`.\n\nTwo things the work turned up that the ticket did not anticipate:\n\n1. `Case.AlertState()` could not simply drop suppression — SPEC §B.3's transition table still needs the four phases or T3/T4 and the suppressed arms of T5/T6 become unreachable. A new unexported `Case.lifecyclePhase()` carries them.\n2. Suppression witnesses never travelled through the state machine at all. They reached the case row but not the in-memory Case, so the projection would have written an empty witness set. `TransitionCommand.SuppressedBy` fixes that.\n\nThe firing-and-silenced assertion (`internal/app/suppression_axis_db_test.go`) was verified honestly: with the old `AlertState()`, CHECK, index and counters temporarily restored, `alerts.state` read `suppressed` and the firing count was 0 against a want of 1. Reverts restored and re-verified.\n\n`alert_cases.suppression_reason`/`suppressed_by` STAY. The timeline does carry `case.suppressed`/`case.unsuppressed`, so dropping them would not have lost history — the forcing argument did not apply. They stay on merit: the Alert's pair is the live axis, the Case's is one firing's record, and a silence's time window can mute episode 3 and not episode 4.\n\nDone-when 7 shipped as `docs/adr/0041-the-alert-case-allocation-and-the-rule-that-decides-it.md` plus `test/scope/allocation_test.go`, which reads both `state` CHECK member sets out of `pg_get_constraintdef` and enforces no ack-stem column on `alerts` and no snooze-stem on either table. `TestAllocationGateFires` plants three columns and re-widens both enums in a rolled-back transaction to prove it has teeth.\n\n3464 tests pass. The one failure is `test/design/TestNoStateHueInChrome` on `web/src/components/glyphs.tsx`, pre-existing from 59c7def and untouched here.","files":null}]}